@@ -156,7 +156,7 @@ Three signals together tell you what happened.
 
 1. **Fanout selection (start of per-fixture phase).** The log line `[api-football] fanout_selection league={league_code} target=… already_complete=… missing_any_endpoint=…` reports the number of in-scope fixtures, how many are already covered for all four per-match endpoints (lineups, events, statistics, players), and how many still miss at least one endpoint.
 
-2. **Completeness check (end of run).** The loader compares **finished** fixture ids in the latest `RAW_APIF_FIXTURES_NEXT` snapshot (`status.short` in `FT`, `AET`, `PEN`) against fanout coverage for each per-match endpoint (`LINEUPS`, `FIXTURE_EVENTS`, `FIXTURE_STATISTICS`, `FIXTURE_PLAYERS`) and emits `[api-football] ingest_completeness_json={...}`. **Coverage is derived directly from `RAW_APIF_FIXTURE_DETAILS`** — the append-only fanout table that holds one row (one payload) per fetched fixture — so it can never drift out of sync with the actual data. A fixture counts as covered for statistics only when its `statistics` array is non-empty (empty stats are retried within a grace window); lineups and events count as covered once the fixture has been fetched; player stats count as covered once fetched, except while the fixture's second fetch is due (its latest fetch came under 3 days after kickoff and 3 days have passed; see [`data_contract.md`](data_contract.md) "Fixture details"). There is no separate coverage tracking table. API predictions are not ingested (we build our own), so there is no predictions endpoint. Unplayed fixtures (upcoming, in-play, cancelled, postponed, abandoned) are reported separately as `fixture_unplayed_count` and do not fail the check. The field `match_level_tables_cover_all_fixtures` (legacy `all_fanout_complete`) is the overall boolean. This is one slice of "complete" — squad freshness and the other raw tables are covered by source freshness, the ingestion spread model, and running dbt after ingest (see [`data_contract.md`](data_contract.md)).
+2. **Completeness check (end of run).** The loader compares **finished** fixture ids in the latest `RAW_APIF_FIXTURES_NEXT` snapshot (`status.short` in `FT`, `AET`, `PEN`) against fanout coverage for each per-match endpoint (`LINEUPS`, `FIXTURE_EVENTS`, `FIXTURE_STATISTICS`, `FIXTURE_PLAYERS`) and emits `[api-football] ingest_completeness_json={...}`. **Coverage is derived directly from `RAW_APIF_FIXTURE_DETAILS`** — the append-only fanout table that holds one row (one payload) per fetched fixture — so it can never drift out of sync with the actual data. A fixture counts as covered for statistics only when its `statistics` array is non-empty (empty stats are retried within a grace window); lineups and events count as covered once the fixture has been fetched; player stats count as covered once fetched, except while the fixture's second fetch is due ([`data_contract.md`](data_contract.md) "Fixture details"). There is no separate coverage tracking table. API predictions are not ingested (we build our own), so there is no predictions endpoint. Unplayed fixtures (upcoming, in-play, cancelled, postponed, abandoned) are reported separately as `fixture_unplayed_count` and do not fail the check. The field `match_level_tables_cover_all_fixtures` (legacy `all_fanout_complete`) is the overall boolean. This is one slice of "complete" — squad freshness and the other raw tables are covered by source freshness, the ingestion spread model, and running dbt after ingest (see [`data_contract.md`](data_contract.md)).
 
    When `match_level_tables_cover_all_fixtures` is `false` during a multi-day backfill, the process exits `3` under the default `API_FOOTBALL_FAIL_ON_INCOMPLETE=1`. Set that env var to `0` until coverage catches up, or skip the check entirely with `API_FOOTBALL_SKIP_COMPLETENESS_CHECK=1` (not recommended long-term).
 
